    "slug": "somalia-marks-flag-day-honoring-designer-mohamed-awale-liban",
    "summary": "On October 12, Somalia celebrates its national flag and remembers Mohamed Awale Liban, the scholar who designed the iconic blue flag with a white star.",
    "content": "Somalia observed National Flag Day on October 12, commemorating the adoption of its national emblem and honoring Mohamed Awale Liban, the scholar credited with designing the blue flag featuring a central white star. The flag, which became a symbol of Somali unity and independence, was created as the nation prepared for self-rule after decades of colonial administration.\n\nAwale Liban was born in 1919 in Galkayo, a town in the Mudug region, during a period when Somali territories were divided under Italian, British, and French colonial rule. He was part of a generation of educated Somalis who advocated for independence and national identity. His design was chosen in the lead-up to Somalia's independence in 1960, and the flag was officially hoisted when the Republic of Somalia was established.\n\nThe blue field of the flag is said to represent the sky and the Indian Ocean that borders the Somali coast, while the five-pointed white star symbolizes the five regions where ethnic Somalis reside: Italian Somaliland, British Somaliland, French Somaliland (now Djibouti), the Ogaden region of Ethiopia, and the Northern Frontier District of Kenya. The flag quickly gained popularity as a unifying emblem for Somalis everywhere.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, Flag Day carries deep meaning. It serves as a reminder of shared heritage and the aspirations of a people scattered across the globe. Many diaspora communities organize cultural events and gatherings on October 12 to celebrate their identity and reflect on the nation's history. Awale Liban's contribution is often highlighted in these observances, underscoring the role of individuals in shaping national symbols.\n\nAwale Liban's legacy extends beyond the flag. He was a prominent educator and public servant who worked in various capacities after independence. His design remains one of the most recognizable African flags, and it continues to inspire pride among Somalis at home and abroad. This year's commemoration included ceremonies in Mogadishu and other cities, as well as virtual events hosted by diaspora groups.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/12/kumuu-ahaa-maxamed-cawaale-liibaan-ninkii-naqshadeeyay-calanka-soomaaliya/",
