    "content": "At least 109 migrants, among them children, lost their lives after the boat they were traveling in capsized near the coast of Djibouti, according to a statement from the African Union. The tragedy occurred on a route frequently used by migrants from the Horn of Africa seeking to reach the Arabian Peninsula.\n\nThe Chairperson of the African Union Commission, Mahmoud Ali Youssouf, expressed deep sorrow over the incident and extended condolences to the families and relatives of the victims. In a statement released by the African Union, he also conveyed solidarity with all those affected by the disaster.\n\nYoussouf emphasized the need to strengthen cooperation among countries in the region and with the international community to address the underlying causes of irregular migration. The incident highlights the persistent dangers faced by migrants who undertake perilous sea journeys in search of better opportunities.\n\nDjibouti, located at the strategic Bab-el-Mandeb strait, serves as a key transit point for migrants from Somalia, Ethiopia, and Eritrea attempting to reach Yemen and other Gulf states. Many flee conflict, poverty, and climate-related hardships, only to encounter life-threatening conditions at sea.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this tragedy resonates deeply, as many families have relatives who have taken similar routes. The loss of over a hundred lives underscores the urgent need for safe, legal pathways and expanded humanitarian support to prevent further deaths.\n\nThe African Union’s call for coordinated action reflects growing concerns about the scale of migration-related fatalities in the region. As authorities continue to respond to the aftermath, the focus remains on supporting survivors and addressing the factors that compel people to risk their lives.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/12/midowga-afrika-oo-war-ka-soo-saaray-geerida-109-qof-oo-tahriibayaal-ah-oo-dooni-kula-degtay-xeebaha-jabuuti/",
